# Python patterns

Narrows `core-common-rules`' design guidance to idiomatic Python 3.12/3.13. Applies only to `*.py`/`*.pyi` files.

## Data and typing

- Model a fixed set of related fields with `@dataclass` (or `attrs` if the project already depends on it), not a `dict` with string keys.
- Use `TypedDict` only for a dict shape that must stay a `dict` (e.g. a JSON payload), not for data your own code constructs.
- Use `typing.Protocol` rather than a concrete base class when the caller only needs structural compatibility.
- Use `Generic[T]`/`class Box[T]:` (3.12+) for a container whose element type varies, not `Any` plus casts.

## Control flow and resources

- Acquire any closable/lockable resource with `with`/`async with`; never rely on `del`, GC or a manual `.close()`.
- Write a custom context manager with `@contextlib.contextmanager` unless the class needs other methods.
- Inside an `except` block, raise the new exception `from` the one being handled.
- Prefer early return over nested `if`/`else` for guard conditions.

## Async

- Group related concurrent awaitables with `asyncio.TaskGroup` (3.11+), not manual task lists with `asyncio.gather`.
- Treat `asyncio.CancelledError` as a signal to clean up and re-raise; it subclasses `BaseException`, so a bare `except:` or `except BaseException` must re-raise it.
- Never call a blocking function (`requests.get`, `time.sleep`, synchronous file I/O, a CPU-bound loop) inside an `async def`; use the async client, `asyncio.sleep`, or `asyncio.to_thread`/an executor.

## Iteration and collections

- Prefer a generator or generator expression over an intermediate list when the result is iterated once.
- Use `itertools`/`collections` (`defaultdict`, `Counter`, `chain`, `groupby`) instead of hand-rolled equivalents.
- No mutable default argument (`def f(items=[])`); default to `None`.

## Logging

- Use the standard `logging` module (or the project's structured logger), not `print`, beyond a throwaway script.
- Pass interpolation arguments to the logging call (`logger.info("got %s", value)`), not an f-string.

## Anti-patterns to flag, not introduce

- Wrapping a whole function body in `try/except Exception: pass`.
- Reusing one name for two unrelated purposes in a function, or reassigning a loop variable's type mid-loop.
- Deep inheritance chains built to share a few methods; prefer composition or a `Protocol` unless the hierarchy is a genuine is-a.
